nominal GDP=real GDP + inflation. When I plot nominal GDP against real GDP and CPI I see that the rise in nominal GDP is almost entirely explained by rise in CPI, I think we have an inflation problem, to which I think energy cost is a big contributor bc core CPI is less elevated. The two wars have severely impaired the global oil reforming supply chain due to destruction and disrepair of equipment esp in Russia. Diesel impacts transportation costs of everything. So yes I attribute at least some of the rising nominal GDP to energy costs. Accelerating real GDP growth is a hypothesis.

